M8.3 HDCD live decoding verification on a burned test disc: terminal output assembled from the saved logs
Redacted for publication: the serial number in output UIDs is shown as <redacted>.
Date: 2026-11-03. Base commit: 852732f79e5619aca02b5252251d299aee3580d4 (M8.4), plus the documentation changes committed with this log. No code changed for the blocks before the addendum; the addendum, at the end, ran after the display fix it describes.
Disc: a CD-RW burned for this test from FFmpeg's FATE sample hdcd-mix.flac (9 s of HDCD with gain adjustment; its licence was not verified, so the disc is for private testing only and nothing derived from its audio is kept here).
  7 gapless tracks, 01:06. Tracks 1-4, 6 and 7 are the sample (9 s each). Track 5 is 3 s of digital silence followed by the sample (12 s), to test an effect that first appears after the 2-second detection hold.
Drive 4295330750: PIONEER BD-RW BDR-XS07U, media 4295332407. Output: Sony Walkman NW-A306 in USB DAC mode, UID AppleUSBAudioEngine:Sony:WALKMAN:<redacted>:1.
CD Player was not running during the blocks before the addendum (checked before the first run and after the last). Every engine run sent zero-valued samples. Captures and decodes were written to a private temporary folder and deleted; only text is kept.
Checksums are FNV-1a as PCMChecksum defines them. References were computed offline from the decode of the captures (FFmpeg's and HDCDCheck's are identical), chained across tracks in the order the engine plays them.
Sections marked "summary" were written from the session notes because the raw output was not saved; every other block is the tool's output, saved at the time or (where stated) re-run from saved files.

=== Burn (summary; the raw drutil output was not kept) ===
Source: seven 16-bit/44.1 kHz stereo AIFF files (01.aiff to 07.aiff) made with ffmpeg from the sample. Checked when this log was assembled: files 01-05 and 07 decode to PCM with MD5 dc91004a7aaa2a5e421b6aa780a18dbd, the sample's own; file 06 is 3 s of digital silence followed by the sample (12 s).
The blank disc the owner inserted was a CD-RW (4x), not the CD-R planned.
A test burn (drutil burn -test, otherwise the options of the real burn below) ejected the disc although -mount was given, and left an empty reserved track on the CD-RW. The owner reinserted the disc and approved a quick erase.
$ drutil -drive 1 erase quick   -> afterwards drutil status showed "No Media Inserted" for about 25 s, then the blank disc.
$ drutil -drive 1 burn -audio -pregap 0 -speed 4 -noverify -mount <folder>   -> completed, no eject.
drutil did not keep the order of the files: the 12 s file (06.aiff) became track 5, not track 6.

=== Read-only check of the burned disc (summary; the raw OpticalCheck and capture output was not kept) ===
Drive 4295330750, media 4295332407, not mounted. Audio CD of 7 tracks; playback timeline 4950 sectors, 2910600 frames (01:06). Track start LBAs 0, 675, 1350, 2025, 2700, 3600, 4275: track 5 has 900 sectors (12 s), the other six 675 (9 s).
$ AudioCheck --capture-pcm 4295330750 <track> 0 <9 or 12> <new.wav>   (tracks 1 to 7; 396900 frames each, track 5 529200)
Every capture: 0 C2-flagged sectors.
Bit-exactness, checked when this log was assembled: the seven captures joined in track order (2910600 frames) equal the burned stream delayed by exactly 667 frames, and the first 667 captured frames are zero. That is the read offset usually quoted for Pioneer drives (not looked up for the XS07U). The engine and the captures read the same disc through the same drive, so the delay is common to both and does not affect the comparisons below.

