=== The click at some track starts: what a run begins with ===
Owner-reported (above and in the control below): a click at the start of track 5 in the first listening and at the start of track 3 in the re-test; none at the start of track 1, and none at the start of track 2 of the ordinary CD.
The drive's read offset is 667 frames (the bit-exactness paragraph of "Read-only check of the burned disc" above: the captures equal the burned stream delayed by exactly 667 frames, and the first 667 captured frames are zero), and the app plays what the drive returns. So a run that starts at the first sector of track 2 to 7 begins with the last 667 frames (15.1 ms) of the track before it, while on track 1 the first 667 frames are zeros. Every track of this disc ends with the end of the sample, which is measured here when this addendum was written (the sample is the data of tracks 1-4, 6 and 7, and of track 5 after its 3 s of silence):
$ cd /tmp/cdplayer-m8-click   (a private folder with the sample as raw 16-bit stereo PCM and the FATE flac; deleted afterwards)
$ md5 sample.s16le
MD5 (sample.s16le) = dc91004a7aaa2a5e421b6aa780a18dbd
$ ffmpeg -v error -i hdcd-mix.flac -f s16le -acodec pcm_s16le -ac 2 -ar 44100 - | md5
dc91004a7aaa2a5e421b6aa780a18dbd
$ cat /tmp/cdplayer-measure.py
import array
import sys

samples = array.array('h')
with open('sample.s16le', 'rb') as f:
    samples.frombytes(f.read())
if sys.byteorder == 'big':
    samples.byteswap()
left, right = samples[0::2], samples[1::2]
frames = len(left)
RATE = 44100
OFFSET = 667
print('frames', frames, 'seconds', frames / RATE)

lead = 0
while lead < frames and left[lead] == 0 and right[lead] == 0:
    lead += 1
print('leading all-zero frames: %d = %.3f s' % (lead, lead / RATE))

tail = slice(frames - OFFSET, frames)
peak = max(max(abs(x) for x in left[tail]), max(abs(x) for x in right[tail]))
print('peak abs over last %d frames: %d = %.1f %% of full scale (32768)' % (OFFSET, peak, 100 * peak / 32768))
print('first frame of tail:', left[frames - OFFSET], right[frames - OFFSET],
      ' last frame:', left[-1], right[-1])
print('overall peak:', max(max(abs(x) for x in left), max(abs(x) for x in right)))

trail = 0
while trail < frames and left[frames - 1 - trail] == 0 and right[frames - 1 - trail] == 0:
    trail += 1
print('trailing all-zero frames:', trail)
print('silence before the music on track 5 (3 s more): %.3f s' % ((3 * RATE + lead) / RATE))
$ python3 /tmp/cdplayer-measure.py
frames 396900 seconds 9.0
leading all-zero frames: 20137 = 0.457 s
peak abs over last 667 frames: 25438 = 77.6 % of full scale (32768)
first frame of tail: 6953 5761  last frame: -5100 -1571
overall peak: 32767
trailing all-zero frames: 0
silence before the music on track 5 (3 s more): 3.457 s
So a run from a track start plays a 15.1 ms burst that begins and ends mid-waveform (the first frame of the tail is 6953 and 5761, the last -5100 and -1571, with silence on either side) and peaks at 77.6% of full scale, then 0.457 s of silence (3.457 s on track 5), then the music. A run that plays through a track boundary has no such burst: the stream is contiguous there (the captures joined in track order equal the delayed stream). The owner's reports fit this. No run without the burst was tried, so it is not proven to be what was heard.

