Reading the output: every eject request of the session came 26 to 70 ms after a click in CD Player, and no request came without one. The options match the app's eject (DiskEjectRequest.swift: DADiskUnmount with kDADiskUnmountOptionWhole, 0x1, then DADiskEject with kDADiskEjectOptionDefault, 0x0), and the 20:26:56 eject is the one the owner made with the app's Eject button. The log does not name the button clicked. My reading, not an owner report: the fourth button is Stop while playing and Eject when stopped, so at 20:12:21 it was clicked twice (Stop at .248, logged at .299, then Eject at .781), and at 20:10:01, with playback stopped since 20:09:16, one click ejected. A third process seen in those seconds, DiskUnmountWatcher (/System/Library/PrivateFrameworks/KerberosHelper/Helpers/DiskUnmountWatcher), logged only "DiskUnmountWatcher: callback" lines, from 20:10:02.032, after the eject was under way (summary of a log show of 20:10:00 to 20:10:03, not kept).

=== Follow-up: the screen on track 5 runs, from screenshots (2026-11-03 21:37) ===
The owner approved a short app playback of the test disc (WALKMAN at minimum volume) and screenshots of the CD Player window only. CD Player was rebuilt from the working tree first (xcodebuild as in DEVELOPMENT.md: BUILD SUCCEEDED) and launched fresh (PID 51787); the test disc was in the drive as media 4295339422 (read-only OpticalCheck: 7 tracks, lead-out 4950, track 5 at LBA 2700, the table of contents above). A helper using CGWindowListCopyWindowInfo gave the player window's number, 43656, and this loop took the shots, each named with its time to the millisecond:
$ python3 /tmp/cdplayer-shoot.py   (repeats: screencapture -x -o -l 43656 /tmp/cdplayer-shots/<HHMMSS.mmm>.png, then a 0.2 s sleep)
