What those shots show, read by me from the images (pairs differing only by a fade between texts or by the controls' enabled state are folded together; the shots were kept outside the repository):
  Before run A (21:37:10): CD DETECTED, 44.1 kHz / 16-bit source, TRK 07 01:06.
  Run A: PREPARING (35.788), BUFFERING (38.062), then CD PLAYING with 44.1 kHz / 16-bit source (39.480, a single shot), then HDCD PLAYING with 44.1 kHz / 16-bit HDCD (from 39.833) unchanged to 51.250, where TRK 06 shows with 44.1 kHz / 20-bit HDCD. After Stop (53.495): HDCD DETECTED, 20-bit HDCD.
  Run B: HDCD DETECTED, 20-bit HDCD before Play (25.933); PREPARING (26.301) and BUFFERING (27.029) keep 20-bit HDCD; at 28.821, as BUFFERING fades into HDCD PLAYING, the text reads 16-bit HDCD and stays so (29.179, the same band as run A's 40.186) until TRK 06 at 40.837 with 20-bit HDCD. After Stop (41.935): HDCD DETECTED, 20-bit HDCD.
Reading the shots against the code (my reading): the detection hold falls inside the 2 s prefill, so the status line reads PREPARING or BUFFERING through it, and the source text keeps the previous run's depth (16-bit source on a fresh disc in run A, 20-bit HDCD after run A in run B). On a disc not yet known as HDCD the hold ends with "no HDCD" (track 5's first 2 s are silence), so run A's first playing shot reads CD PLAYING and 16-bit source; HDCD PLAYING follows when the producer, reading ahead, reaches track 5's first effect at 4.456 s and reports it. That matches the "not yet known" row of the status table in HDCD_ROADMAP.md. Before the level drop, both runs read 16-bit HDCD, and 20-bit HDCD appeared only at track 6, where decoding starts; run B's carried 20-bit text showed only before any audio played.

=== Follow-up: why track 2 of the ordinary CD had no click (read-only, 2026-11-03 21:43) ===
With the owner's approval the control disc went back in and CD Player was quit; pgrep showed no CDPlayer, AudioCheck or OpticalCheck process (summary). A read-only OpticalCheck showed the control disc's table of contents again (now media 4295340633: 10 audio tracks, lead-out 191445, track 2 at LBA 21880). The capture reads the drive as the app does, so its first 667 frames are what a run from the start of track 2 plays first: the last 667 frames of track 1.
$ AudioCheck --capture-pcm 4295330750 2 0 1 /tmp/cdplayer-ctl/track2-start.wav
Drive 4295330750: PIONEER BD-RW  BDR-XS07U, media 4295340633; track 2, pre-emphasis false; sectors 21880..<21955.
Speed: original 4233 kB/s; requested 176 kB/s; reported 4233 kB/s.
Read 75 sectors (44100 frames); checksum f96bb5a458fc4d69; 55.8 kB/s.
C2: 0 sectors flagged.
PASS: original drive speed restored and verified at 4233 kB/s.
Wrote /tmp/cdplayer-ctl/track2-start.wav: 176400 PCM bytes. It holds the disc's audio; delete it after analysis.
$ python3 /tmp/cdplayer-ctl/measure.py   (reads the WAV's 16-bit stereo samples after its 44-byte header; reports peak and RMS over frames 0-666, the first and boundary frames, the largest frame-to-frame step, and peak and RMS after frame 667)
frames 44100
first 667 frames (end of track 1): peak 3526 = 10.76 % FS (-19.4 dBFS), rms 972.5 (-30.6 dBFS)
first frame: 1315 1204  frame 666: 641 580  frame 667: 659 1011
largest frame-to-frame step in frames 1..667: 756
all-zero frames among the first 667: 0
frames 667..<5077 (track 2): peak 32459 = 99.06 % FS, rms 9095.9 (-11.1 dBFS)
frames 667..<44100 (track 2): peak 32459 = 99.06 % FS, rms 9197.4 (-11.0 dBFS)
The WAV was deleted after this run.
Reading the output: a run from the start of this track 2 also begins with 15.1 ms of the track before it, but that tail peaks at 10.8 % of full scale (against 77.6 % on the test disc), starts at 1315 and 1204 (against 6953 and 5761), and runs without a break into track 2, whose first 100 ms is already at -11.1 dBFS RMS. On the test disc the louder burst is followed by 0.457 s of silence, so it stands alone. My reading: on this disc the 15 ms tail is quiet and merges into the music, so there is nothing separate to hear as a click.

=== Not covered ===
Peak Extend lifting real peaks above 16-bit full scale: this sample flags it but barely uses it, so a disc that uses it is still needed.
The texts on the track 5 runs before the level drop and during the detection hold are now recorded from screenshots ("Follow-up: the screen on track 5 runs" above), on one fresh run and one run after a decoded run; the owner added no notes on those two runs.
The AudioCheck "decoded:" line follows the engine's snapshot, so after the fix it follows the same rule with no change to the tool. That was exercised on an effectual disc by the offline test of the engine only; no AudioCheck window was re-run on the test disc after the fix. The control run used the rebuilt tool on a disc without HDCD, which reads "decoded: no" as before.
That the 667-frame burst at a track start is what the owner heard as a click: it fits the measurement and the reports, including the quiet, unbroken tail measured on track 2 of the ordinary CD ("Follow-up: why track 2 ..." above), but no run without the burst was tried.
The 6 dB level difference by ear: the owner reported the decoded audio as clearly quieter, with clean sound. The factor itself is checked by sample values only (Offline decode section above): no-effect samples decode to exactly sample x 32768, against sample x 65536 undecoded.
